Z||000000||stimpy||10-28-2001||06:57 PM||||I have had my @home service since last Thursday and so far have had about 8 hours total where it has been up and synching. Just a little while ago, I accidently shut of the power and restarted it. Tech support says I may have fried the modem. Grrrrrr. <P>Are there any websites like dslreports.com that specialize in cable modem hookups? I'm looking for advice to get me better performance, etc. Who else has cable modem service running to their homes? Is my experience unusual or par for the course?||||reg||8
Z||000001||Phil||10-28-2001||07:12 PM||||I have @ home  and have had no  big problems so far ( about 1 year). Had to reset the modem about twice. Did you rent the modem or buy it?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mach10||10-28-2001||07:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by stimpy:<BR><B>I have had my @home service since last Thursday and so far have had about 8 hours total where it has been up and synching. Just a little while ago, I accidently shut of the power and restarted it. Tech support says I may have fried the modem. Grrrrrr. <P>Are there any websites like dslreports.com that specialize in cable modem hookups? I'm looking for advice to get me better performance, etc. Who else has cable modem service running to their homes? Is my experience unusual or par for the course?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>First of all:   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"><BR>you can't fry the modem by powering it down<P>What kind of modem? Need brand/model.<P>||||reg||5
Z||000003||sqoach||10-28-2001||07:52 PM||||That's what I was thinking Mach10. I didn't think the modem would fry just by turning off the power. Hell, if it did, we'd have to buy about 2 dozen modems.<BR>We've had cable modem for about 3 years. No major problems that I can think of. One time when I put in a new cd-writer drive, I bumped the modem out of place a bit, and I couldn't get a connection. Plugged it back in place and it worked fine.<P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">              <BR>^^^^^<B>new project</B>^^^^^<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><LI>86 SC 2.5L 5spd</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||10-28-2001||08:17 PM||||Splitters are bad news for cable modems. Each one it goes thru looses a little signal strength. Some splitters are better than others. Some of the cheap ones loose a LOT of signal strength just going thru one.<P>I found all this out when mine quit working after a month. The cable in this house has grown over the years. It was running thru 3 splitters to get to the modem. I did some rerouting so it's only 2 and it's been working since. One of these days I'm going to rewire the whole thing...||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||10-28-2001||08:18 PM||||It shouldn't take a cable modem longer than a few minutes to get online. What it actually does is scan for the proper up- and downstream frequencies. And frying your modem because you resetted it is indeed complete and utter  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif">.<P>If the modem can't get online in a few minutes, there's something else wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000006||DRH||10-28-2001||08:20 PM||||Oh, <A HREF="http://www.speedguide.net" TARGET=_blank>www.speedguide.net</A>  has some info and I believe an analyzer. There's another really good one somewhere but I can't find the link.||||reg||1
Z||000007||stimpy||10-28-2001||08:20 PM||||It's a Toshiba PCX1100U. We bought it from the cable company but it's still under warranty so they are going to replace it if anything is wrong with it. I thought that story about it being fried sounded a bit odd. A tech comes out tomorrow morning, so we'll see what happens. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||10-28-2001||08:32 PM||||Hmmm.... Ok you aren't one of ours  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I believe that the toshi *should* have a reset switch on it... hold that down for 6 seconds to get a firmware reboot.<P>Failing that, cut the power for 2 minutes. <BR>make sure all the connections are nice and tight. <BR>What most likely happened is that something croaked in the area... If the modem trashed out, then it wasn't from something that YOU did...<BR>The other possibility is a DHCP server problem. The modem needs an IP, too. If it has a bad BOS key, then it'll be nice and happy AS LONG AS IT DOESN'T HAVE TO RENEW. By powering it off, you are forcing a renew on it...<BR>Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||stimpy||10-28-2001||08:44 PM||||Mach10, you the emeffin MAN! I had tried to reset the modem a couple of times at the direction of the tech support guy, but no go. However, he only had me disconnect for about 1 minute. I unplugged it for 2 minutes, and whaddaya know? I'm cooking with gas again!||||reg||14
Z||000010||Mach10||10-28-2001||09:21 PM||||Normally, one would say "That's why I get paid the big bucks"<P>But I make ****  all for @home tech  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>Stimpy: Here's your fee: You must make only American jokes for ONE week  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>That'll give us cannucks a break  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000011||Dgzfiero||10-28-2001||10:25 PM||||Hey Stimpy welcome to the wonderful world of @home cable modems . Mine was doing the same thing. It turned out that the line was in overdrive(not a good thing like in a car). The service guys came out and hooked up some meters,talked to each other and said it was a line problem and turned in a work order to do some stuff outside. After that it started working right.||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||10-29-2001||03:32 PM||||I got cable roadrunner and it locked up my puter every other time i used it, messed up my outgoing email, It was also slower than my dial up. I ordered Direct TV DSL, see if thats anybetter. I had the roadrunner taken back out in 4 days, got them to credit me back for it.||||reg||1
Z||000013||btoth||10-29-2001||04:34 PM||||I really like our new Speakeasy service. (DSL)  They actually send us emails keeping us up to date on network stuff, financial info, etc.  It turns out that unlike a lot of the cheaper companies, they're actually making money and plan on staying in business.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>We got this service because it was the only other one that offered a static ip (actually 2!) and fast enough uploads... 128kbps sucks, I want 400kbps!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  and 1.5mbps down.  This was after Telocity (Rhythms) went out of business.<P>Our service has only been down once, and that was because our NY connection went down on 9/11.  Now it's back up, and in the meantime, they connected us to Seatle wihtout a hitch.||||reg||1
Z||000014||stimpy||10-29-2001||05:45 PM||||Well, it went out 2 more times today. Grrr. At least Cox cable is very good at getting someone out right away. Apparently, like Dougs service, my line is overdriving by about 15db, which apparently is enough to disturb weather patterns and kill small animals. Dude did some adjustments and it's back on the road (knock on wood). Plus, the cable guy was really impressed with the Fiero, heheh.<P>As far as DSL goes, I didn't even seriously consider that, as the only company offering DSL is Southwestern Bell, aka the KIngs of Empty Promises. As a former SBC employee, I wouldn't dream of giving them any additional cash. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Mach10||10-30-2001||02:27 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rogergarrison:<BR><B>I got cable roadrunner and it locked up my puter every other time i used it, messed up my outgoing email, It was also slower than my dial up. I ordered Direct TV DSL, see if thats anybetter. I had the roadrunner taken back out in 4 days, got them to credit me back for it.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>ROFL<P>Sounds like you got some USELESS techs<P>1) Lockups while using aren't related to the service... It's the NIC... Just based on the way the technology works. If it's crashing, it's internal PC hardware. ALWAYS. The DOCSIS modems operate totally independantly of the system itself. All they do is act like a router. they pass traffic. They don't "talk" to the PC.<BR>2) Slow speeds, like what you are mentioning are not characteristic of the service... Something else is FUBAR.<P>I get SO many calls like this. I ain't road-runner, and I ain't Cox... But I am very familiar with the system... I only have THIS to say:<P>1) @home techs are generally AWFUL. Too quick to send out techs. Too quick to ignore the problem itself, and guess at the solution<BR>2) Noone seems to understand that by fncking up ONCE sets the scene all too well. Roger, here, now hates the RR service, 'cos some ass-bandit couldn't diagnose the problem correctly.<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000016||87GTZ34||10-30-2001||10:19 AM||||Roger,  WARNING!!!   I am dumping my directv DSL.  After they started reconfiguring the telocity network (they purchased telocity), they decided to cut back to 128K (a fast modem speed) transfer rates but not cut back on subscription price.  I was getting 1.3 something before as I am very close to the DSLAM and now I'm at 1/10 of that! Ouch!  Check out the newsgroup directvdsl.support for a lot more on the topic.<P>Stimpy - dslreports has a tuning section which should enhance your internet experience even more (in layman terms, it allows bigger and therefore fewer chunks to xfer data) by reducing overhead.  CAUTION don't use the dslreports' pre-made register updates!  My strong recommendation is that you either use the browser or print a hard copy and use regedit to edit the registers step-by step yourself.  They have a note somewhere something to the effect that they are not responsible if your computer breaks...<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 10-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||87GTZ34||10-30-2001||10:20 AM||||Roger - what operating system are you running?||||reg||3
